We need to write an assert statement that captures this specification. The method is `public E remove(int index)`. It returns `oldValue` which is the element at that index before removal. The precondition is that index is valid, i.e., `Objects.checkIndex(index, size);` which throws if invalid. So the specification is: If index is valid (i.e., 0 <= index < size), then the result equals the element at that index before removal, i.e., \old(this.get(index)).

We need to refer to the original element before removal. Since we have observer method `get(int index)`. Use \old(this.get(index)). Also need to assert that index is valid: 0 <= index && index < \old(this.size())? Actually after removal size will be decreased by 1. So the old size is \old(this.size()). Valid index condition is index >=0 && index < \old(this.size()). So we can assert: (0 <= index && index < \old(this.size())) => \result.equals(\old(this.get(index)))? Since result is of type E, we need to compare equality. In Java spec, we can use `\result == \old(this.get(index))` for reference equality, but better to use `.equals`. In examples they used `.equals` for objects. Eg: `this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i)))`. For result, we can write `\result.equals(\old(this.get(index)))`. However need to ensure that `\result` is not null? But we can just use `.equals`. Could also use `\result == \old(this.get(index))` if same reference. We'll follow pattern: `\result.equals(\old(this.get(index)))`.

